h (away) are anti? In Newman along C2-Cβ, front C3 wedge (toward viewer) and back OH dash (away from viewer) are actually same side? Need clarify. In a Newman projection, front atom substituents are drawn as lines from center; back atom substituents as lines from circle. Anti (180°) means front substituent points up, back substituent points down (opposite directions in projection). Wedge at front (toward viewer) corresponds to up? Dash at back (away) corresponds down? In a line drawing, wedge at C2 and dash at Cβ are not necessarily anti; they are both out of plane but opposite directions (wedge toward viewer, dash away) so they are anti? Yes, wedge and dash on adjacent atoms with bond in plane: if wedge at front carbon points up/out, dash at back carbon points down/out? Dihedral 180? I think wedge and dash are anti if they are on opposite sides of the bond (one up, one down). So okay.

