Bicyclo[4.2.2]decane: bridges lengths 4,2,2 -> atoms excluding bridgeheads=8 +2=10. Edges = (4+1)+(2+1)+(2+1)=5+3+3=11. V=10. cycles=11-10+1=2. So two rings. Product 2 C14H16O4: unsaturation = (2*14+2-16)/2 = (30-16)/2=7. Two rings + three double bonds =5, plus two ester carbonyls =2 -> total 7. Good.

Product 3: product 2 + cyclopentadiene (C5H6) via Diels-Alder (no loss) -> C19H22O4? C14H16O4 + C5H6 = C19H22O4. Unsaturation = (2*19+2-22)/2 = (40-22)/2=9. Product 2 had 7 (2 rings +3 C=C +2 C=O). Cyclopentadiene has C5H6: unsaturation = (12-6)/2=3 (2 double +1 ring). Diels-Alder consumes two π (diene 2 double? diene has 2 π, dienophile 1 π) and forms two σ + one ring? Net: product 2 (diene) + cyclopentadiene (dienophile) consumes 3 π (diene two + dienophile one) and forms one new ring (cyclohexene) plus retains one π from diene? Diels-Alder: diene has 2 double bonds -> product has one double bond (between C2,C3 of diene) and two new σ; dienophile double becomes σ. So π count decreases by 2 (diene 2 ->1, dienophile 1->0) = -2. Rings increase by 1 (new cyclohexene) = +1. Unsaturation change: -2 π +1 ring = -1? Product 2 unsat 7 + cyclopentadiene 3 =10; Diels-Alder consumes 2 π and adds 1 ring -> net -1? Actually product unsat = (reactants unsat) -2 (π consumed) +1 (ring formed) =10-1=9. Yes. Product 3 has 9: 2 rings from product2 +1 new ring + remaining double bonds: product2 had 3 C=C; diene C5=C6-C7=C8 becomes one C=C (C6=C7) after Diels-Alder; C2=C3 remains; cyclopentadiene as dienophile had two C=C; one consumed, one remains. So remaining C=C: C2=C3, C6=C7, Cp remaining =3. Rings: product2 2 + new cyclohexene + Cp ring? Cyclopentadiene ring already counted as ring in cyclopentadiene (1) but as dienophile its ring remains? Product2 rings 2 + Cp ring 1 + new ring 1 =4 rings. Carbonyls 2. Total unsat =4+3+2=9. Good. So product 3 has 4 rings, 3 C=C, 2 C=O. Name "octahydrobiphenylene" with ethano/methano bridges: biphenylene skeleton has? Biphenylene C12H8 has 4 rings? Biphenylene: two benzene rings fused to cyclobutene? It has 4 rings? Biphenylene (dibenzo cyclobutadiene) has 4 rings? Actually biphenylene has two benzene rings connected by a cyclobutane ring (central 4-member) -> three rings? Biphenylene C12H8: unsaturation = (24+2-8)/2=9. It has 4? Benzene rings ea